React Synthetic Events, which are React’s built-in event handlers that wrap around native DOM events for consistency across browsers.

Here’s a list of the most common built-in event handler props in React (like onClick, onCopy, onMouseMove, etc.) with their descriptions:

🔹 Mouse Events

onClick → Fires when an element is clicked.

onDoubleClick → Fires when an element is double-clicked.

onMouseEnter → Fires when the mouse pointer enters an element (doesn’t bubble).

onMouseLeave → Fires when the mouse pointer leaves an element (doesn’t bubble).

onMouseOver → Fires when the mouse pointer is moved onto an element (bubbles).

onMouseOut → Fires when the mouse pointer is moved out of an element (bubbles).

onMouseMove → Fires when the mouse pointer is moved inside an element.

onMouseDown → Fires when a mouse button is pressed.

onMouseUp → Fires when a mouse button is released.

🔹 Keyboard Events

onKeyDown → Fires when a key is pressed down.

onKeyUp → Fires when a key is released.

onKeyPress → Fires when a key is pressed (⚠️ deprecated in React, use onKeyDown).

🔹 Clipboard Events

onCopy → Fires when text/content is copied.

onCut → Fires when text/content is cut.

onPaste → Fires when text/content is pasted.

🔹 Form Events

onChange → Fires when the value of an input, textarea, or select changes.

onInput → Fires when the user inputs text (more low-level than onChange).

onSubmit → Fires when a form is submitted.

onReset → Fires when a form is reset.

onInvalid → Fires when a form element fails validation.

🔹 Focus Events

onFocus → Fires when an element (like input) gains focus.

onBlur → Fires when an element loses focus.

🔹 Drag & Drop Events

onDrag → Fires while an element is being dragged.

onDragStart → Fires when dragging starts.

onDragEnd → Fires when dragging ends.

onDragOver → Fires when a dragged item is moved over a valid drop target.

onDragEnter → Fires when a dragged item enters a drop target.

onDragLeave → Fires when a dragged item leaves a drop target.

onDrop → Fires when a dragged item is dropped.

🔹 Touch Events (for mobile devices)

onTouchStart → Fires when a finger touches the screen.

onTouchMove → Fires when a finger moves on the screen.

onTouchEnd → Fires when a finger is lifted off the screen.

onTouchCancel → Fires when a touch event is interrupted.

🔹 Pointer Events (modern alternative to mouse/touch)

onPointerDown → Fires when a pointer (mouse, stylus, finger) is pressed down.

onPointerUp → Fires when pointer is released.

onPointerMove → Fires when pointer moves.

onPointerEnter / onPointerLeave → Similar to mouse enter/leave.

onPointerOver / onPointerOut → Similar to mouse over/out.

onGotPointerCapture / onLostPointerCapture → Handle pointer capturing.

🔹 Media Events

onPlay → Fires when media (audio/video) starts playing.

onPause → Fires when media is paused.

onEnded → Fires when playback ends.

onVolumeChange → Fires when volume changes.

onTimeUpdate → Fires when playback position updates.

onLoadedMetadata → Fires when media metadata is loaded.

🔹 Image Events

onLoad → Fires when an image or script loads successfully.

onError → Fires when image/script fails to load.

🔹 Animation & Transition Events

onAnimationStart → Fires when a CSS animation starts.

onAnimationEnd → Fires when a CSS animation ends.

onAnimationIteration → Fires each time an animation repeats.

onTransitionEnd → Fires when a CSS transition ends.

⚡ Summary:
React has synthetic event props like onClick, onCopy, onMouseMove, onChange, onSubmit, onFocus, onDrag, onTouchStart, onPlay, onLoad, onAnimationEnd, etc.